# Testing Conventions

- Framework: pytest (no pytest-asyncio; use `asyncio.run()` for async tests)
- CI: Python 3.12 + 3.13 matrix on Ubuntu (GitHub Actions)
- Mock HA stubs via `_ensure_ha_stubs()` before importing component modules
- Use `tmp_path` fixture for file-based tests (state file, command file, certificates)
- Always `importlib.reload()` component modules after stubbing to pick up changes
- Test naming: `Test<Class>.test_<behaviour>` — describe the expected outcome

## CI-Safe Path Handling

Never use default system paths (`/ssl`, `/data`) in tests — they require root permissions
on the CI runner. Always pass `tmp_path`-based paths for `cert_path`, `key_path`, and
`status_file` when creating `CertificateManager` instances in tests.

```python
# BAD — fails on CI with PermissionError: [Errno 13] Permission denied: '/ssl'
manager = CertificateManager(
    username="test@example.com", password="pw",
    domain="example.com", email="ssl@example.com",
)

# GOOD — uses pytest tmp_path fixture
manager = CertificateManager(
    username="test@example.com", password="pw",
    domain="example.com", email="ssl@example.com",
    cert_path=str(tmp_path / "fullchain.pem"),
    key_path=str(tmp_path / "privkey.pem"),
    status_file=str(tmp_path / "cert_status.json"),
)
```

## Async Test Pattern

```python
# Typical async test pattern without pytest-asyncio
def test_reads_state_file(self, tmp_path):
    hass = self._make_hass()
    coordinator = _make_addon_coordinator(hass, str(tmp_path / "state.json"), ...)

    async def _run():
        data = await coordinator._async_read_addon_state()
        assert data["current_ip"] == "1.2.3.4"

    asyncio.run(_run())
```
